WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:03.872 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.872 --> 00:00:05.324 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.324 --> 00:00:11.150 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.180 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: And
so from last time,

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:21.050 align:middle line:84%
we covered the Itô formula
for the single case

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:26.330 align:middle line:90%
where we have the integral--

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:31.250 align:middle line:84%
or sorry, the function
of a Brownian motion,

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.300 align:middle line:90%
function of one variable.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.640 align:middle line:84%
And importantly, with this
function of a one variable,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.160 align:middle line:84%
we get that the differential
of the function is equal

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.910 align:middle line:84%
to the first derivative
of the function evaluated

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:52.820 align:middle line:84%
at the Brownian motion plus
1/2 the second derivative

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.000 align:middle line:90%
of the function.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.690 align:middle line:90%
And so this is Itô's formula.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:03.380 align:middle line:84%
And when we apply Itô's
formula in one dimension,

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:11.810 align:middle line:84%
we can consider defining the
antiderivative of the function

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:18.620 align:middle line:84%
F, and that's capital
F. And so Itô's formula

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:25.670 align:middle line:84%
gives us this expression for
the integral of f of Bs dBs.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:34.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is a very useful way
to compute Itô integrals.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:41.240 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we go to functions
of two variables, so case 2,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.240 align:middle line:84%
we're going to have
a function of time.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.970 align:middle line:90%
and Bt, say.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:53.690 align:middle line:84%
When we want to evaluate this
function of time and space

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.130 align:middle line:84%
as a function of
Brownian motion,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:01.160 align:middle line:84%
then our Itô's formula
is expressed this way.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:06.400 align:middle line:84%
We basically have the partials
of little f with respect to t

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.699 align:middle line:84%
and with respect to x and
then a second-order term,

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:14.210 align:middle line:84%
the second partial of
f with respect to x.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is how Itô's
formula generalizes.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's really
convenient to highlight

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.610 align:middle line:84%
is that, with Itô's
formula, we get

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:34.970 align:middle line:84%
this top formula for the
change in value of f of tx.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:44.530 align:middle line:84%
So we have this theorem,
which is for how f of Bt

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.870 align:middle line:84%
changes from an
initial condition.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.010 align:middle line:84%
And in looking at
this expression,

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.080 align:middle line:90%
it's a sum of two terms.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.530 align:middle line:84%
The first term is an integral
with respect to time, ds.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:07.580 align:middle line:84%
And so that function will
be non-random on paths.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.630 align:middle line:84%
The second term is the
integral of the partial

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:18.370 align:middle line:84%
of f with respect to x dBs
And that will be random

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.120 align:middle line:90%
depending on DBs.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:27.140 align:middle line:84%
So if we have this
formula for the increment,

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.780 align:middle line:84%
then we basically
have a constant term

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:37.360 align:middle line:84%
plus an integral of Brownian
motion, which has mean 0

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.230 align:middle line:90%
as a random variable.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:45.800 align:middle line:84%
But if the term that's in
the integrand over time,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:55.600 align:middle line:84%
if that's equal to 0, then this
function f will be a Martingale.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.180 align:middle line:84%
So importantly, in
quantitative finance,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.890 align:middle line:84%
we often are interested in
solving for values of functions

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.820 align:middle line:84%
where the functions
are the price or value

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.080 align:middle line:90%
of a derivative over time.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:17.730 align:middle line:84%
And if we can express it in the
right framework, where the price

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.980 align:middle line:84%
function follows a
Martingale, then it

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.230 align:middle line:90%
will satisfy this PDE condition.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:32.040 align:middle line:84%
Now, the notion of the value
of some derivative contract

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.210 align:middle line:84%
or asset following a
Martingale over time

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:42.220 align:middle line:84%
means that it has a constant
expected value of time.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.820 align:middle line:84%
So we don't have the natural
growth in value according

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.600 align:middle line:90%
to the risk-free rate.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:56.310 align:middle line:84%
So to set this up, we'll need to
normalize the prices discounted

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.090 align:middle line:90%
to the present value.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:03.670 align:middle line:84%
So that comes into play
in different applications.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:10.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, there are some examples
of Itô's formula, applying it.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:14.140 align:middle line:84%
And with the Brownian
motion with drift,

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.500 align:middle line:90%
we went through that last time.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.530 align:middle line:84%
And then, finally,
in this section,

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:25.890 align:middle line:84%
I want to highlight how
geometric Brownian motion is

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:29.430 align:middle line:84%
represented in terms of the
stochastic differential equation

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.090 align:middle line:90%
as given here.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:43.530 align:middle line:84%
So dPt is equal to Pt mu
dt plus Pt sigma dPt dBt.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:50.630 align:middle line:84%
And importantly, we basically
have these factors Pt

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:54.810 align:middle line:84%
on both sides, which could
be divided in both sides.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.020 align:middle line:84%
So we might think
that we're working

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.080 align:middle line:84%
with the derivative
or differential

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.900 align:middle line:90%
of the log of the price.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.080 align:middle line:90%
But it's a little bit different.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.600 align:middle line:84%
So with this geometric
Brownian motion,

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:19.190 align:middle line:84%
if we apply Itô's lemma to
the function log of dt--

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:26.786 align:middle line:84%
so if we think of log of
dt is equal to G of Pt,

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:33.020 align:middle line:84%
t, then the partial
of G with respect to P

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:39.450 align:middle line:84%
is simply 1 over P. The partial
of G with respect to t is 0,

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:45.050 align:middle line:84%
and the second partial of G
with respect to P is minus 1

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.720 align:middle line:90%
over P squared.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:54.020 align:middle line:84%
So Itô's Lemma gives us that
the differential of log P

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.640 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of two
terms, one that's

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:04.400 align:middle line:84%
times dt, the other
that's times sigma dBt.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:11.300 align:middle line:84%
And so log price is going to
follow a generalized Wiener

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:15.590 align:middle line:84%
process with a drift rate given
by mu minus sigma squared over 2

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.880 align:middle line:84%
and variance rate
equal to sigma squared.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:23.240 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's important
about this result

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.370 align:middle line:84%
is that the drift rate
changes from mu to mu

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:32.990 align:middle line:90%
minus sigma squared over 2.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:39.410 align:middle line:84%
So basically the drift
rate on the log scale

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:45.500 align:middle line:84%
has a drag due to the
quadratic variation.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:54.537 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider
normalizing the log return--

00:07:54.537 --> 00:07:56.120 align:middle line:84%
well, actually not
normalizing, but we

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.820 align:middle line:84%
look at an increment of
the log return that's

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.160 align:middle line:90%
normally distributed.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:09.090 align:middle line:84%
And then let's see, if
we consider, I guess,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:14.130 align:middle line:84%
looking at what the conditional
distribution is of log Pt,

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.550 align:middle line:84%
it's given by this
Brownian motion,

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.940 align:middle line:84%
which gives normally
distributed log

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:26.960 align:middle line:84%
values with a particular mean
mu star and variance sigma star.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:34.760 align:middle line:84%
And so the expectation of our
price at some time point capital

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:39.390 align:middle line:84%
T given the value
at time little t,

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:44.570 align:middle line:84%
it'll be a lognormal random
variable with expectation

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:49.390 align:middle line:84%
equal to the initial
price Pt, P sub little t,

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:57.230 align:middle line:84%
times the exponential
of mu times T minus t.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:01.280 align:middle line:84%
There should be an e here,
which is in that factor.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:09.580 align:middle line:84%
So despite there being
a drag on the drift,

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:15.910 align:middle line:84%
the expectation of the
exponential of the log

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.650 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have that
drag due to drift.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:25.450 align:middle line:84%
It actually has the mean
log return in that factor.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:29.900 align:middle line:84%
And with lognormal
random variables,

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.330 align:middle line:84%
we have formulas for
the mean and variance,

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:36.760 align:middle line:84%
which we covered, actually, in
the first couple of lectures.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:43.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, interestingly, if we think
about just what kinds of results

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.750 align:middle line:84%
happen with values
of mu and sigma

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:50.500 align:middle line:84%
that we might expect to
see in financial markets,

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:57.340 align:middle line:84%
here's an example where
we consider a drift rate

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:08.830 align:middle line:84%
mu of 0.3 per year, a time
period for the lognormal return

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:13.520 align:middle line:84%
of one half year from time
little t to capital T,

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.550 align:middle line:84%
and an annualized
volatility of 0.4, or 40%--

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:27.970 align:middle line:84%
so this is perhaps reasonable
for some moderately risky stocks

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:34.450 align:middle line:84%
that have a modest return
expectation of 30% per year

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:38.480 align:middle line:84%
and a volatility
rate of 40% per year.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:45.900 align:middle line:84%
And when we compute the
expectation of the price,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:51.360 align:middle line:84%
It equals the initial
price Pt times 1.1618.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, that 1.1618 says
that we expect the price

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:00.600 align:middle line:90%
to increase by about 16%.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:06.940 align:middle line:84%
And if we think of a half
year and a drift rate of 0.3,

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:12.000 align:middle line:84%
we might have thought it
would be 15% If we didn't

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.390 align:middle line:90%
follow through the details.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:15.460 align:middle line:90%
So it's a bit higher return.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:20.250 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, for the
volatility or standard deviation

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:28.710 align:middle line:84%
of this price, it turns out
that the volatility or variance

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:37.020 align:middle line:84%
of the price at time capital
T is 0.335 squared times

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:38.710 align:middle line:90%
the square of Pt.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:42.210 align:middle line:84%
So the variance is
also a bit higher

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.420 align:middle line:84%
than you might expect if
you just naively interpreted

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:50.220 align:middle line:84%
the mu and sigma as
appropriate annualized values.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:55.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this result,
we can actually

00:11:55.530 --> 00:12:02.550 align:middle line:84%
compute the continuously
compounded percent return

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:06.370 align:middle line:90%
of a geometric Brownian motion.

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:12.900 align:middle line:84%
So we can look at
the change in value

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:17.040 align:middle line:84%
from time little
t to capital T--

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.120 align:middle line:84%
the log of that ratio is
the difference in logs--

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.290 align:middle line:84%
and divide that by the length of
the period, capital T minus t.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:32.520 align:middle line:84%
And we get the annualized rate
of return for the geometric

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:33.940 align:middle line:90%
Brownian motion.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:40.540 align:middle line:84%
And what that rate of return
as a random variable is,

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:45.090 align:middle line:84%
it is normal with mean mu
minus a half sigma squared

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:48.510 align:middle line:84%
and variance sigma
squared over T minus t.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:54.720 align:middle line:84%
So with this, what's
significant or important

00:12:54.720 --> 00:13:03.450 align:middle line:84%
is that the rate of return
over long periods of time

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:07.120 align:middle line:84%
converges to a
constant average rate.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:10.140 align:middle line:84%
And the variance about
that average rate

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:14.850 align:middle line:84%
is going to 0 as the time
into the future continues.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:19.000 align:middle line:84%
So this is an important property
of geometric Brownian motion,

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:28.140 align:middle line:84%
that the growth of
the process basically

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:32.920 align:middle line:84%
grows at a constant rate
with some variability.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:37.040 align:middle line:84%
But that variability
goes to 0 as we consider

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:38.150 align:middle line:90%
very, very long periods.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:42.170 align:middle line:90%


00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:49.040 align:middle line:84%
So here, let's say I have a
simulation of Brownian motion

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.180 align:middle line:84%
where we consider
the same mu and sigma

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.600 align:middle line:84%
as the previous
slides, and we consider

00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:00.620 align:middle line:84%
a time period of capital
T equal to four years.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:06.890 align:middle line:84%
And we define increments
on that interval.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:12.800 align:middle line:84%
And look at, basically, Brownian
motion with the appropriate mu

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.930 align:middle line:84%
and sigma, then geometric
Brownian motion,

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:20.750 align:middle line:84%
which is the exponential of
that Brownian motion, and then

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:22.380 align:middle line:90%
the log returns.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.300 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look
at what we have.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:32.126 align:middle line:90%


00:14:32.126 --> 00:14:36.740 align:middle line:84%
So here's some simulated
paths of Brownian motion

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:40.130 align:middle line:84%
with drift with those
mu and sigma parameters.

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:44.570 align:middle line:84%
And importantly, we
have the mean return

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:48.230 align:middle line:84%
as a function of time,
or the mean value

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.630 align:middle line:84%
as a function of time
of the Brownian motion

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.440 align:middle line:90%
being given by the blue line.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:56.630 align:middle line:84%
And then plus or minus
2 standard deviations

00:14:56.630 --> 00:15:00.500 align:middle line:90%
is given by the red curves.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:04.860 align:middle line:84%
And if we simulate not
five but, say, 50 paths,

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:10.010 align:middle line:84%
this is what this Brownian
motion looks like.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.060 align:middle line:90%
And if we--

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.150 align:middle line:84%
I guess, before doing
this, I'd like everyone

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:20.120 align:middle line:84%
to just think about how is this
set of paths going to transform

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:22.770 align:middle line:84%
when we take the
exponential of them,

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:26.600 align:middle line:84%
so basically the exponential
of Brownian motion

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.990 align:middle line:84%
with constant
drift and variance?

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:34.780 align:middle line:84%
Well, here's the
geometric Brownian motion.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:39.130 align:middle line:84%
And what I've done is I've
drawn the same blue line

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:42.470 align:middle line:84%
and the same red lines
for the Brownian motion

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:46.840 align:middle line:84%
and how that translates
to the exponential scale.

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:50.650 align:middle line:84%
And what's rather
striking here is

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:57.760 align:middle line:84%
that there are paths
out of 50 that really

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:02.950 align:middle line:84%
have extraordinary
performance, and so

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:09.310 align:middle line:84%
basically growing in value
from by a factor of 10,

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:14.110 align:middle line:84%
or an order of 10,
basically to an order of 20

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:16.340 align:middle line:90%
on the exponential scale.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:21.100 align:middle line:84%
And so what I think is an
interesting takeaway from this

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:25.300 align:middle line:84%
is that, if we have a simple
Brownian motion process defining

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:31.850 align:middle line:84%
the log price processes,
then with reasonably

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:36.180 align:middle line:84%
modest drift and
volatility parameters,

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:42.600 align:middle line:84%
there can be some realizations
that have phenomenal returns.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.950 align:middle line:84%
And so when you look at the
price returns of stocks,

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:49.730 align:middle line:84%
often our attention
gravitates towards those

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:54.750 align:middle line:84%
that have extraordinary
returns, like NVIDIA or Tesla,

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:58.800 align:middle line:84%
say, over some
periods in the past,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.360 align:middle line:90%
maybe not so much recently.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:05.030 align:middle line:84%
But these extraordinary
returns over a number of years

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:09.530 align:middle line:84%
is actually consistent
with the possibility

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:13.680 align:middle line:84%
of geometric Brownian motion
models underlying those.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:20.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we look at the
average log return,

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:25.250 align:middle line:84%
we get this graph
for these 50 paths

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:32.130 align:middle line:84%
just doing the time
series of volatilities

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:37.110 align:middle line:84%
of the-- or sorry, of
returns, average returns

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.280 align:middle line:90%
over across each of the paths.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:47.250 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at zooming in on
that path, this is the space--

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:49.560 align:middle line:84%
or it's an illustration
of the realizations

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:51.880 align:middle line:84%
of the average
returns over time.

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:58.890 align:middle line:84%
So over short periods, we can
expect very different return

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.230 align:middle line:90%
magnitudes, perhaps.

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:06.040 align:middle line:84%
But as the time period for
measuring returns increases,

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:11.510 align:middle line:84%
it stabilizes to vary
about this constant level.

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:14.490 align:middle line:90%


00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:16.220 align:middle line:90%
So let's go back here.

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:19.870 align:middle line:90%


00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:27.300 align:middle line:84%
So with stochastic calculus,
there are many follow-on topics.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:30.150 align:middle line:84%
We're going to focus today
on stochastic differential

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:31.950 align:middle line:90%
equations.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:36.870 align:middle line:84%
But it's important to know
that stochastic calculus

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:40.450 align:middle line:84%
and differential equations
apply to other problems,

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:46.530 align:middle line:84%
such as population growth
and filtering problems,

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:48.700 align:middle line:90%
optimal stopping problems.

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:55.560 align:middle line:84%
And so it's a very rich set
of applications of the theory.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:19:01.890 align:middle line:84%
So let's go to stochastic
differential equations.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.250 align:middle line:90%
These slides are a bit dense.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:09.250 align:middle line:84%
But I've color-coded
some of the pieces

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:12.060 align:middle line:84%
so we can go through
it more slowly.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:17.970 align:middle line:84%
So let's go through the
derivation of the Black-Scholes

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.240 align:middle line:90%
differential equation.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:22.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, you've already seen
this a few times in the class

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:24.910 align:middle line:84%
from a guest lecturer and
from Vasily's lecture,

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:28.380 align:middle line:84%
but I think it bears
repeating here.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:33.570 align:middle line:84%
If we have an asset
price that follows

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:41.730 align:middle line:84%
geometric Brownian motion,
so dPt is equal to mu--

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:48.480 align:middle line:84%
Pt mu dt plus Pt
sigma, dBt, then

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.720 align:middle line:84%
let's consider the price
of a derivative that's

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:54.240 align:middle line:90%
contingent on Pt.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:58.110 align:middle line:90%
And as a motivating example--

00:19:58.110 --> 00:19:59.470 align:middle line:90%
say, a call option--

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:05.800 align:middle line:84%
but we could consider arbitrary
options or derivatives.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:13.350 align:middle line:84%
And if we apply Itô's lemma
to the price function Gt,

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:19.050 align:middle line:84%
then Itô's lemma gives
us this sum of two terms,

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:24.440 align:middle line:84%
dt times the partial of G
with respect to P mu Pt,

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:29.240 align:middle line:84%
partial of G with respect to
dt, the second partial of G with

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:36.020 align:middle line:84%
respect to P times 1/2 times
sigma squared Pt squared,

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:41.420 align:middle line:84%
and then, finally, this partial
of G with respect to P times

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:43.670 align:middle line:90%
sigma Pt dBt.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:50.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we think about
discretizing the price

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:54.660 align:middle line:84%
dynamics of the
stock or the asset,

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:59.310 align:middle line:84%
as well as the derivative,
then we can say,

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:02.840 align:middle line:84%
OK, the change or
increment in Pt

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:12.840 align:middle line:84%
is a mean growth rate times
delta t plus sigma Pt delta Bt.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:19.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's a random term and a
deterministic term given time

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:20.870 align:middle line:90%
little t.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:26.010 align:middle line:84%
And if we do the same for the
increment of the derivative,

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:30.890 align:middle line:84%
we then basically
substitute into dG,

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:34.380 align:middle line:90%
delta t's and delta B's.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:38.630 align:middle line:84%
So we get the same
constant factor times delta

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:46.730 align:middle line:84%
t plus dG dP times
sigma Pt delta Bt.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:56.240 align:middle line:84%
And the brilliant argument
for solving for the option

00:21:56.240 --> 00:22:00.650 align:middle line:84%
price or derivative
price is to consider

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:08.720 align:middle line:84%
constructing a portfolio Vt
that eliminates dependence

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.580 align:middle line:90%
on the Brownian motion.

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:15.390 align:middle line:84%
So if we eliminate dependence
on the Brownian motion,

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:21.670 align:middle line:84%
then we basically have a
deterministic price function

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:25.370 align:middle line:90%
for the portfolio.

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:29.270 align:middle line:84%
So one way of doing
this is to say,

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:36.400 align:middle line:84%
let us short one
derivative, so minus Gt.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:43.240 align:middle line:84%
If we sell the derivative at
price Gt, we receive Gt dollars.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:51.430 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to add
to that a number of shares

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:55.240 align:middle line:90%
of the asset given by dG dP.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.850 align:middle line:90%


00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:02.110 align:middle line:84%
So if we basically
take minus 1 times

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:09.760 align:middle line:84%
this plus the dG
dP times the asset,

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:16.570 align:middle line:84%
then the increment of
value of this portfolio

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:21.820 align:middle line:84%
is simply delta t
times this factor.

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:28.300 align:middle line:84%
And we've canceled
out the terms in green

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:35.140 align:middle line:84%
when multiplied by minus 1
for the derivative or dG dP

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:37.220 align:middle line:90%
for the asset.

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:43.450 align:middle line:84%
So we end up getting a
portfolio whose change in value

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:47.860 align:middle line:84%
depends just on
this bracketed term

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:53.560 align:middle line:84%
here, with no Brownian
motion increment involved.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:24:02.470 align:middle line:84%
And so having eliminated
the risk in the portfolio,

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:09.040 align:middle line:84%
arbitrage arguments say
that Vt must be risk-free.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:13.210 align:middle line:84%
And as a risk-free
asset equivalent,

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:16.580 align:middle line:84%
it should earn the
riskless interest rate.

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:20.580 align:middle line:84%
So our change in value
of this portfolio

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:25.260 align:middle line:84%
is simply the initial
value times the rate

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:30.960 align:middle line:84%
of return of the riskless
asset over the period delta t.

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:35.340 align:middle line:84%
And so what we can
do then is equate

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:41.700 align:middle line:84%
the two expressions for what
the increment in Vt should be.

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:46.210 align:middle line:84%
If it's riskless, here's
the right-hand side.

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:50.860 align:middle line:84%
And according to
our Itô's formulas,

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:54.690 align:middle line:84%
the portfolio has an
increment value given

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.920 align:middle line:90%
by this factor of delta t.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:25:02.920 align:middle line:84%
So if we eliminate
delta t from both sides,

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:08.040 align:middle line:84%
we get this formula, which
is called the Black-Scholes

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:10.540 align:middle line:90%
differential equation.

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:14.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's really
significant is

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:20.430 align:middle line:84%
that at least when one studies
Black-Scholes theory in finance

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:25.380 align:middle line:84%
classes, one thinks
of call options

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:30.060 align:middle line:84%
and deriving their price or put
options through put call parity.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:34.020 align:middle line:84%
What's really important here
is that this Black-Scholes

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:38.910 align:middle line:84%
differential equation applies
for those vanilla options,

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:45.270 align:middle line:84%
but also for any contingent
claim derivative where

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:52.290 align:middle line:84%
the derivative-- where the
financial derivative Gt

00:25:52.290 --> 00:26:00.900 align:middle line:84%
basically depends upon the
value of the underlying asset.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:04.730 align:middle line:84%
So we have Gt, which
is a contingent claim.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:07.740 align:middle line:90%


00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:20.850 align:middle line:84%
And we assume that Gt is equal
to some function f of Pt and t.

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:27.990 align:middle line:84%
So we assume that the
derivative depends only

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:34.350 align:middle line:84%
on the current level of the
asset and the current time.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:36.717 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:26:36.717 --> 00:26:38.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So they won a
Nobel Prize for it, right?

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:43.860 align:middle line:84%
What part of the paper was
the Nobel Prize-winning part?

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:46.360 align:middle line:84%
Was is it the construction
of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:46.360 --> 00:26:49.560 align:middle line:90%
or was it the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:53.880 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: It
really was the extensions

00:26:53.880 --> 00:27:00.780 align:middle line:84%
of the initial papers to
handle modeling derivatives

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:05.680 align:middle line:90%
under no arbitrage assumptions.

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:13.130 align:middle line:84%
And so the basic theory extends
amazingly to a broad range

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:14.520 align:middle line:90%
of applications.

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.580 align:middle line:90%
And so yeah.

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:23.150 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the initial papers
were rather impressive

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:31.850 align:middle line:84%
in terms of solving these
pricing problems using analysis,

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:32.990 align:middle line:90%
basically.

00:27:32.990 --> 00:27:37.400 align:middle line:84%
And so the initial papers were
really quite mathematical.

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:41.300 align:middle line:84%
And since then, arguments
and developments

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:44.760 align:middle line:84%
have been made that make
it more straightforward.

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:48.260 align:middle line:84%
And what we're showing here
is essentially the benefits

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:50.595 align:middle line:90%
from those later developments.

00:27:50.595 --> 00:27:51.095 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:27:51.095 --> 00:27:54.350 align:middle line:90%


00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:58.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is every [INAUDIBLE]
changing what the portfolio is?

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:00.380 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: I'm
sorry, can you say--

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:06.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like, the amount of
the asset changes over time.

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:12.590 align:middle line:84%
So then I'm not seeing the value
of the portfolios, like Pt.

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:14.270 align:middle line:84%
Since the amount
of Pt has changed,

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:22.010 align:middle line:84%
then they bought or sold
some of it, [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:22.010 --> 00:28:23.480 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
Well, OK, I guess

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:27.500 align:middle line:84%
this equation should
be interpreted

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:31.700 align:middle line:84%
in terms of just infinitesimal
increments delta t.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:38.430 align:middle line:84%
And as you point
out, as time evolves,

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:41.540 align:middle line:84%
the value of the
contract evolves.

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:49.140 align:middle line:84%
The partial derivatives of the
asset with respect to time,

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:50.640 align:middle line:90%
those are not constant, as well.

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:56.670 align:middle line:84%
So as the process increments,
those will also change.

00:28:56.670 --> 00:29:04.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a simplification,
I guess, of the argument.

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:07.550 align:middle line:84%
But if we're looking
at what properties

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:13.900 align:middle line:84%
must hold in the infinitesimal
time range, then this is exact.

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:15.350 align:middle line:90%
But OK.

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:17.185 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:29:17.185 --> 00:29:22.480 align:middle line:84%
Why can we always say that Vt
equals minus Vt plus that term

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:25.398 align:middle line:90%
after t beta [INAUDIBLE]?

00:29:25.398 --> 00:29:28.065 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Oh, why can we
say that this equals this term?

00:29:28.065 --> 00:29:34.586 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The second
to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:34.586 --> 00:29:36.490 align:middle line:90%


00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:38.565 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK,
just repeat the question.

00:29:38.565 --> 00:29:41.878 align:middle line:90%


00:29:41.878 --> 00:29:46.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the expression
for Vt at the top, how do--

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:48.170 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] for all t's?

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:53.410 align:middle line:90%


00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:54.520 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK.

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:58.210 align:middle line:84%
You're saying that the Vt
equaling minus Gt plus--

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:05.710 align:middle line:84%
OK, that's going to be the
initial value of the portfolio

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:08.170 align:middle line:90%
at time little t.

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:13.960 align:middle line:84%
So at time little
t, we are combining

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:18.840 align:middle line:90%
a short derivative claim.

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:21.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So we just ignore
what happened before.

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:24.080 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Exactly, we
ignore what happens before.

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:26.800 align:middle line:84%
But this highlights
in a good way--

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:30.970 align:middle line:84%
it's a good question--
that, when t evolves--

00:30:30.970 --> 00:30:34.040 align:middle line:84%
or the time involves
past time little t,

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:37.450 align:middle line:84%
then the partial of
G with respect to P

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:44.990 align:middle line:84%
at that future time point will
not necessarily equal this.

00:30:44.990 --> 00:30:48.760 align:middle line:84%
And in order to have a portfolio
that eliminates the Brownian

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:53.920 align:middle line:84%
motion term, one would need
to adjust the number of shares

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:57.350 align:middle line:90%
in the asset.

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:05.050 align:middle line:84%
So if we go back
to this argument

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:08.650 align:middle line:84%
here, where we sell
short one derivative

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:13.480 align:middle line:84%
and buy this many
shares at time little t,

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:17.390 align:middle line:84%
this cancels out
the Brownian motion.

00:31:17.390 --> 00:31:23.360 align:middle line:84%
But then for increments
of time t or time after t,

00:31:23.360 --> 00:31:27.290 align:middle line:84%
we still are short
the derivative.

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:32.140 align:middle line:84%
But we may need to adjust the
number of shares of the asset

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:35.240 align:middle line:90%
to buy to hedge that derivative.

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:38.680 align:middle line:84%
And that's where delta
hedging comes into play.

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So that
expression doesn't include--

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.380 align:middle line:84%
if we're not assuming
that we've done this

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:43.960 align:middle line:90%
through time 0 [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.220 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: No,
not at all, not at all.

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:46.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:53.440 align:middle line:84%
And it's helpful just to see,
OK, if, at any arbitrary time t,

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:56.860 align:middle line:84%
you want to consider
buying the or selling

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:58.610 align:middle line:84%
the derivative,
valuing the derivative,

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:01.810 align:middle line:90%
how should that be valued?

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:02.530 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:03.170 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:09.360 align:middle line:84%
So what's important
with this Black-Scholes

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:12.300 align:middle line:84%
partial differential
equation is that there

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:15.390 align:middle line:84%
are different boundary
conditions that

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:20.620 align:middle line:84%
vary by the derivative
that we are working with.

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:26.100 align:middle line:84%
And so with call options, we
have basically the final value

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:29.340 align:middle line:84%
at time capital T. The
maturity of the option

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:35.100 align:middle line:84%
is given by the hockey
stick function of Pt minus k

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:37.800 align:middle line:90%
if that's greater than 0 or 0.

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:40.210 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, for
the put option,

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:43.740 align:middle line:90%
it has this terminal value.

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:49.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's, as I said, very
powerful about this theory

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:55.230 align:middle line:84%
is that the partial differential
equation, the Black-Scholes

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:59.800 align:middle line:84%
partial differential equation
is satisfied by any derivative,

00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:05.070 align:middle line:84%
so long as there is no
arbitrage in the market.

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:11.070 align:middle line:84%
And so with different
derivatives contracts,

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:15.600 align:middle line:84%
there will be different
boundary conditions on those.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:18.750 align:middle line:84%
So what we now want
to do is figure out

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:23.410 align:middle line:84%
how to solve these partial
differential equations.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:32.310 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to do is go
through the diffusion equation.

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:36.540 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that
the Black-Scholes

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:38.220 align:middle line:84%
partial differential
equation can

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:41.700 align:middle line:84%
be transformed into
this diffusion equation

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:46.950 align:middle line:84%
by changing the scale of time,
reversing time, and doing

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:50.410 align:middle line:90%
some other normalizations.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:53.340 align:middle line:84%
So this diffusion
equation is very

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:57.330 align:middle line:84%
fundamental to solving
the Black-Scholes

00:33:57.330 --> 00:33:59.260 align:middle line:90%
partial differential equations.

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:03.620 align:middle line:84%
And it's the simplest form
of this second-order partial

00:34:03.620 --> 00:34:08.840 align:middle line:84%
differential equation that
we can study and understand

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:10.110 align:middle line:90%
its solutions.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:17.420 align:middle line:84%
So the heat equation
is an equation

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:22.530 align:middle line:84%
that is satisfied
by a function u

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:26.210 align:middle line:84%
if the partial of
u with respect to t

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:30.949 align:middle line:84%
is equal to lambda times the
second partial of u with respect

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:32.489 align:middle line:90%
to x squared.

00:34:32.489 --> 00:34:35.453 align:middle line:90%
So this is the heat equation.

00:34:35.453 --> 00:34:44.989 align:middle line:90%


00:34:44.989 --> 00:34:54.260 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to give
an example here which is

00:34:54.260 --> 00:34:56.070 align:middle line:90%
very important and technical.

00:34:56.070 --> 00:35:01.460 align:middle line:84%
But in terms of what kinds of
functions satisfy this diffusion

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:09.560 align:middle line:84%
equation, we actually went
through looking at the density

00:35:09.560 --> 00:35:22.820 align:middle line:84%
of a Gaussian distribution,
which is equal to 1 over root 2

00:35:22.820 --> 00:35:31.460 align:middle line:84%
pi root t e to the minus
1/2 x squared over t.

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:39.320 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of a Gaussian
density, which basically

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:45.590 align:middle line:84%
is a bell-shaped curve around
0 with some standard deviation

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:58.480 align:middle line:84%
here given by 1, this would
be the density perhaps for t

00:35:58.480 --> 00:36:04.760 align:middle line:90%
equals 1, the density function.

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:09.340 align:middle line:84%
And this is the density of
a Brownian motion starting

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:12.290 align:middle line:90%
at time 0, ending at time t.

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:20.920 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider increasing
time, then the change in P--

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:26.200 align:middle line:84%
So P sub t is equal to
lambda times P sub xx.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:29.740 align:middle line:90%


00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:37.420 align:middle line:84%
And so the change over time is
proportional to the curvature

00:36:37.420 --> 00:36:39.370 align:middle line:90%
of the density.

00:36:39.370 --> 00:36:43.790 align:middle line:84%
And so if you look back in the
course notes, course materials,

00:36:43.790 --> 00:36:50.560 align:middle line:84%
we looked at how the density
of Brownian motion over time,

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:55.220 align:middle line:84%
it drops where there's
strong negative curvature,

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:58.730 align:middle line:84%
and it rises where there's
positive curvature.

00:36:58.730 --> 00:37:05.150 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically get flatter
curves as time increases.

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:11.080 align:middle line:84%
So the normal density function
follows this heat equation.

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:16.180 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to
see is that functions

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:19.510 align:middle line:84%
that follow the heat
equation can actually

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:25.030 align:middle line:84%
be represented as linear
combinations of these Gaussian

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:26.470 align:middle line:90%
densities.

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:31.390 align:middle line:84%
So the special case of the
Gaussian density is, in a sense,

00:37:31.390 --> 00:37:36.190 align:middle line:84%
very, very general in terms of
defining solutions to the heat

00:37:36.190 --> 00:37:37.720 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:46.690 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider, I guess,
how the heat equation is often

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:47.870 align:middle line:90%
set up.

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:51.790 align:middle line:84%
It says OK, let's consider the
temperature in a long, thin bar

00:37:51.790 --> 00:37:55.600 align:middle line:84%
of material that's perfectly
insulated, so temperature only

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:59.150 align:middle line:84%
varies with distance
along the bar at time t.

00:37:59.150 --> 00:38:07.645 align:middle line:90%


00:38:07.645 --> 00:38:15.640 align:middle line:84%
So mu of xt is the value of
the temperature over time.

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:25.060 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider an initial
value for the heat in the bar

00:38:25.060 --> 00:38:31.360 align:middle line:84%
given by u not of x,
then what we want to do

00:38:31.360 --> 00:38:37.330 align:middle line:84%
is solve for how that
heat function changes.

00:38:37.330 --> 00:38:46.510 align:middle line:84%
And so some really
neat properties to use

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:49.930 align:middle line:84%
are similar properties
to what we have

00:38:49.930 --> 00:38:53.830 align:middle line:84%
when we're trying to solve for
ordinary differential equations.

00:38:53.830 --> 00:39:01.950 align:middle line:84%
So first observation is, suppose
we have two solutions, u1

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:02.770 align:middle line:90%
and u2.

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:06.250 align:middle line:84%
Then the sum of those is
going to also be a solution.

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:09.000 align:middle line:90%
So solutions are linear.

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:18.240 align:middle line:84%
And if we have a collection of
solutions indexed by little s,

00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:22.500 align:middle line:84%
we can consider
taking a weighted sum

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:26.140 align:middle line:90%
of those per an integral.

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:29.100 align:middle line:84%
And this will also
be a solution.

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:35.970 align:middle line:84%
So the linearity of
the solution spaces

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:42.060 align:middle line:84%
extends to this integral
of solutions indexed

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:44.413 align:middle line:90%
by s also being a solution.

00:39:44.413 --> 00:39:48.690 align:middle line:90%


00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:50.790 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:58.800 align:middle line:84%
So the simplest
initial value problem

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:05.010 align:middle line:84%
is to consider this u
function to be a Dirac delta

00:40:05.010 --> 00:40:23.790 align:middle line:84%
function at x equals 0, so u
of x0 equaling the Dirac delta

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:28.325 align:middle line:90%
function at t equals 0.

00:40:28.325 --> 00:40:31.230 align:middle line:90%


00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:38.430 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider this
function, which is essentially

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:49.340 align:middle line:84%
the PDF of a normal with mean
0 and variance 2 lambda t,

00:40:49.340 --> 00:40:59.900 align:middle line:84%
then this density function
turns out to solve the heat--

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:02.340 align:middle line:90%
or satisfy the heat equation.

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:05.450 align:middle line:84%
But it also satisfies
the initial condition,

00:41:05.450 --> 00:41:12.740 align:middle line:84%
where, when we let t go to 0,
then it equals the Dirac delta

00:41:12.740 --> 00:41:13.590 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:17.040 align:middle line:84%
So what's the Dirac
delta function?

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:19.603 align:middle line:90%
It's equal to 0 for--

00:41:19.603 --> 00:41:24.620 align:middle line:90%


00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:27.980 align:middle line:90%
it equals 0 for x0 equal to 0.

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:42.170 align:middle line:84%
And it equals-- and the integral
of u of x0 dx is equal to 1.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:50.360 align:middle line:84%
So it's a function that's
it's positive but has weight

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:52.970 align:middle line:90%
under the integral equal to 1.

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:58.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is a rather abstract
kind of function to work with.

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:04.040 align:middle line:90%
And actually, I don't know if--

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:06.235 align:middle line:84%
is anyone here familiar
with Paul Dirac?

00:42:06.235 --> 00:42:08.810 align:middle line:90%


00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:12.920 align:middle line:84%
There's a great-- he was
a physicist at Cambridge

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:14.210 align:middle line:90%
University.

00:42:14.210 --> 00:42:16.710 align:middle line:84%
And if you're familiar
with, I guess,

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:21.810 align:middle line:84%
Schrödinger and
other physicists,

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:24.200 align:middle line:84%
he's like number three
or four in the list

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:26.280 align:middle line:90%
of top physicists at the time.

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:31.010 align:middle line:84%
And he came up with this
Dirac delta function

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:34.080 align:middle line:90%
as something worth working with.

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:37.310 align:middle line:84%
So it's, in a sense,
an imaginary function.

00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:44.110 align:middle line:84%
It's basically the limit of
this normal density function

00:42:44.110 --> 00:42:50.540 align:middle line:90%
as lambda or as t goes to 0.

00:42:50.540 --> 00:42:55.180 align:middle line:84%
So it becomes totally
concentrated at 0.

00:42:55.180 --> 00:42:59.800 align:middle line:84%
So with this
function, u delta, we

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:07.270 align:middle line:84%
have this particular function
satisfying the heat equation

00:43:07.270 --> 00:43:09.170 align:middle line:90%
with that initial condition.

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:19.150 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we want to generalize
from this initial function

00:43:19.150 --> 00:43:22.760 align:middle line:84%
or the Dirac delta
function at 0,

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:28.330 align:middle line:84%
suppose we consider
wanting to solve

00:43:28.330 --> 00:43:33.380 align:middle line:84%
where the initial conditions
are given by a function u0 of x.

00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:37.030 align:middle line:84%
So in this discussion,
we can think

00:43:37.030 --> 00:43:44.020 align:middle line:84%
of u0 of x, the
initial value, being

00:43:44.020 --> 00:43:47.560 align:middle line:90%
some arbitrary known function.

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:49.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's like the
boundary conditions

00:43:49.750 --> 00:43:54.370 align:middle line:90%
of this function at time 0.

00:43:54.370 --> 00:44:05.560 align:middle line:84%
And if we integrate our u0
function with the Dirac delta

00:44:05.560 --> 00:44:10.660 align:middle line:84%
function evaluated at x
minus s, then we simply

00:44:10.660 --> 00:44:16.450 align:middle line:90%
get back this u0 function at x.

00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:19.450 align:middle line:84%
So the Dirac delta
function allows

00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:27.170 align:middle line:84%
us to pull out in an integral
the u0 function itself.

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:30.760 align:middle line:84%
So there's an identity
relationship between u0

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:35.380 align:middle line:84%
and this transform of u0 with
the Dirac delta function.

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:43.964 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we consider
different solutions.

00:44:43.964 --> 00:44:47.800 align:middle line:90%


00:44:47.800 --> 00:44:56.443 align:middle line:84%
We can index solutions to
this heat equation by delta--

00:44:56.443 --> 00:44:59.500 align:middle line:90%


00:44:59.500 --> 00:45:02.380 align:middle line:90%
or by s, rather.

00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:07.270 align:middle line:84%
And then we can integrate this
u delta function with respect

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:14.540 align:middle line:84%
to the factor u0 of s, and we
get this formula for u of xt.

00:45:14.540 --> 00:45:20.920 align:middle line:84%
So for any initial
condition function, u0 of s,

00:45:20.920 --> 00:45:27.580 align:middle line:84%
this integral will,
in fact, give us

00:45:27.580 --> 00:45:36.130 align:middle line:84%
the solution function u whose
initial value u of x0 equals u0.

00:45:36.130 --> 00:45:39.460 align:middle line:84%
And so in order
for this to work,

00:45:39.460 --> 00:45:44.220 align:middle line:84%
we need to have the
integrals existing.

00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:47.563 align:middle line:90%
And if we have--

00:45:47.563 --> 00:45:50.250 align:middle line:90%


00:45:50.250 --> 00:45:56.700 align:middle line:84%
if the integrals exist, and we
can take partial derivatives

00:45:56.700 --> 00:46:00.720 align:middle line:84%
inside the integral
sign, then we end up

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.990 align:middle line:84%
getting the partial
of u with respect to t

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:09.420 align:middle line:84%
is the integral of the partial
of u delta with respect to dt.

00:46:09.420 --> 00:46:12.960 align:middle line:84%
That's the only part
that depends on t.

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:18.310 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, the partial with
respect to x, second partial,

00:46:18.310 --> 00:46:20.490 align:middle line:84%
we can take that under
the integral sign

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:23.950 align:middle line:90%
and get this expression here.

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:27.990 align:middle line:84%
So if this holds, then
we have this function

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:29.710 align:middle line:90%
satisfying the heat equation.

00:46:29.710 --> 00:46:33.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, under what
conditions can we

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:38.941 align:middle line:84%
differentiate integrals
underneath the integral sign?

00:46:38.941 --> 00:46:41.790 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you
remember from calculus,

00:46:41.790 --> 00:46:44.855 align:middle line:84%
with real calculus,
how that works.

00:46:44.855 --> 00:46:47.610 align:middle line:90%


00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:48.840 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:49.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:46:49.990 --> 00:46:54.855 align:middle line:90%


00:46:54.855 --> 00:46:56.230 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
Say that again.

00:46:56.230 --> 00:47:00.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] bit
about, when does ux t converge?

00:47:00.580 --> 00:47:02.080 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
When it converges?

00:47:02.080 --> 00:47:06.180 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:47:06.180 --> 00:47:16.620 align:middle line:84%
OK, well, ux of t will exist
if u0 is sufficiently smooth.

00:47:16.620 --> 00:47:23.520 align:middle line:84%
And so if the initial conditions
are sufficiently smooth,

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:27.820 align:middle line:84%
then their first derivatives and
second derivatives are bounded.

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:31.020 align:middle line:84%
And so these
integrals are bounded,

00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:33.610 align:middle line:84%
and they're
absolutely integrable.

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:35.630 align:middle line:84%
And so we're able
to take derivatives

00:47:35.630 --> 00:47:37.380 align:middle line:90%
underneath the integral sign.

00:47:37.380 --> 00:47:45.530 align:middle line:84%
So when you have unbounded
functions u0 that

00:47:45.530 --> 00:47:48.320 align:middle line:84%
maybe are sufficiently
smooth, then 1

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:52.860 align:middle line:84%
can take limits as the bound
on that function increases.

00:47:52.860 --> 00:47:57.600 align:middle line:84%
And if those limits exist,
then these solutions apply.

00:47:57.600 --> 00:48:01.640 align:middle line:84%
But what's important
here is to see

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:08.990 align:middle line:84%
that we have this u0 of
x is equal to this sum

00:48:08.990 --> 00:48:17.520 align:middle line:84%
of the Dirac deltas
multiplied by u0 of s value.

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:20.420 align:middle line:84%
So it's a weighted
sum of Dirac delta.

00:48:20.420 --> 00:48:30.800 align:middle line:84%
And the equation for the
sum of these or integral

00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:35.400 align:middle line:84%
of those weighted by u0 of s is
given by this expression here.

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:39.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we can differentiate
underneath the integral sign,

00:48:39.510 --> 00:48:47.372 align:middle line:84%
then basically the
values of the--

00:48:47.372 --> 00:48:52.820 align:middle line:84%
or the u function
can be represented

00:48:52.820 --> 00:48:58.790 align:middle line:84%
as in a Taylor series with
the terms in the Taylor series

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:02.370 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to
these differentials.

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:08.780 align:middle line:84%
And they-- each of those
satisfy the heat equation alone.

00:49:08.780 --> 00:49:12.950 align:middle line:84%
So let's see how
this works with where

00:49:12.950 --> 00:49:15.903 align:middle line:90%
u0 is an indicator function.

00:49:15.903 --> 00:49:18.860 align:middle line:90%


00:49:18.860 --> 00:49:21.640 align:middle line:90%
So we're thinking of--

00:49:21.640 --> 00:49:25.840 align:middle line:90%


00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:26.340 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:29.900 align:middle line:90%
Let me just write here.

00:49:29.900 --> 00:49:36.560 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:49:36.560 --> 00:49:42.730 align:middle line:84%
For this description,
I want to actually

00:49:42.730 --> 00:49:47.590 align:middle line:84%
think about, ultimately,
a Brownian motion process

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:54.310 align:middle line:90%
xt that starts at 0.

00:49:54.310 --> 00:49:59.020 align:middle line:84%
And so we have different
paths, possible paths,

00:49:59.020 --> 00:50:02.740 align:middle line:84%
for this Brownian
motion process.

00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:10.240 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to be
interested in, basically,

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:21.430 align:middle line:84%
an indicator for the path lying
between the intervals a and b.

00:50:21.430 --> 00:50:29.770 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider a u0 function
for the initial condition

00:50:29.770 --> 00:50:37.420 align:middle line:84%
to be the indicator of whether
a process lies between a and b.

00:50:37.420 --> 00:50:44.020 align:middle line:84%
So there will be some paths that
start with the indicator being 1

00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:47.950 align:middle line:84%
and others with the
indicator being equal to 0.

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:59.500 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that the
expectation of u0, the indicator

00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:02.770 align:middle line:84%
function with respect
to this Gaussian density

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:07.510 align:middle line:84%
is simply the probability
of lying between a and b.

00:51:07.510 --> 00:51:13.400 align:middle line:84%
So u of xt, as
defined in the top,

00:51:13.400 --> 00:51:18.680 align:middle line:84%
is simply the expectation
of the indicator function,

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:22.450 align:middle line:84%
which is the probability
of lying between a and b.

00:51:22.450 --> 00:51:27.130 align:middle line:84%
And we can solve
for this probability

00:51:27.130 --> 00:51:32.020 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the
cumulative normal CDF,

00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:36.730 align:middle line:84%
standard normal, evaluated at
the upper limit minus the mean

00:51:36.730 --> 00:51:41.860 align:middle line:84%
divided by the standard
deviation minus the CDF

00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:44.350 align:middle line:84%
at the lower limit
minus the mean divided

00:51:44.350 --> 00:51:46.010 align:middle line:90%
by the standard deviation.

00:51:46.010 --> 00:51:49.630 align:middle line:84%
So this u of xt, which
is the difference

00:51:49.630 --> 00:51:57.670 align:middle line:84%
of two functions of Gaussian
densities, integrals of them,

00:51:57.670 --> 00:52:01.750 align:middle line:90%
is a solution to this problem.

00:52:01.750 --> 00:52:06.923 align:middle line:90%
So now, in the next slide--

00:52:06.923 --> 00:52:09.700 align:middle line:90%


00:52:09.700 --> 00:52:12.820 align:middle line:90%
whoops OK, so that solution.

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:15.820 align:middle line:90%


00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:21.400 align:middle line:84%
All right, so the
next set of concepts

00:52:21.400 --> 00:52:25.600 align:middle line:84%
we want to introduce you to
are solving these equations

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:31.140 align:middle line:84%
using properties of
similarity and invariance.

00:52:31.140 --> 00:52:38.100 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to be
considering changes in the time

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:40.000 align:middle line:90%
and space variables.

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:44.580 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have time variable,
and here we have the space

00:52:44.580 --> 00:52:48.100 align:middle line:90%
variable with Brownian motions.

00:52:48.100 --> 00:52:53.640 align:middle line:84%
And so what if we
were to rescale time

00:52:53.640 --> 00:53:03.600 align:middle line:84%
to a tau which is
equal to alpha t,

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:09.690 align:middle line:84%
and we were to rescale
x to a variable y, which

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:11.733 align:middle line:90%
is equal to some beta x.

00:53:11.733 --> 00:53:14.280 align:middle line:90%


00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So we're just changing
the units of time

00:53:17.310 --> 00:53:23.850 align:middle line:84%
and the units of space
in a constant way.

00:53:23.850 --> 00:53:32.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we consider a v
function which equals the u

00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:44.460 align:middle line:84%
function, then we
basically can map u into v

00:53:44.460 --> 00:53:49.343 align:middle line:84%
by redefining u in
terms of y and--

00:53:49.343 --> 00:53:54.150 align:middle line:90%


00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:58.960 align:middle line:84%
sorry, not u in terms
of y, u in terms of tau.

00:53:58.960 --> 00:54:09.690 align:middle line:84%
And so if we apply the chain
rule to ut, we get alpha vt.

00:54:09.690 --> 00:54:13.320 align:middle line:84%
And if we apply the chain
rule to the partial of u

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:17.170 align:middle line:84%
with respect to
x, we get beta vy.

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:23.690 align:middle line:84%
And the second partial
of u with respect to y

00:54:23.690 --> 00:54:27.570 align:middle line:90%
is beta squared vyy.

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:36.230 align:middle line:84%
And so we end up getting our
initial heat equation in x and t

00:54:36.230 --> 00:54:44.570 align:middle line:84%
to be this equation
in terms of tau and y.

00:54:44.570 --> 00:54:52.230 align:middle line:84%
And if alpha equals
beta squared--

00:54:52.230 --> 00:54:58.040 align:middle line:84%
so we could arbitrarily,
if we knew the--

00:54:58.040 --> 00:55:01.560 align:middle line:84%
if we're free to choose
alpha and beta arbitrarily,

00:55:01.560 --> 00:55:04.170 align:middle line:84%
we could choose alpha
equal to beta squared.

00:55:04.170 --> 00:55:09.770 align:middle line:84%
And we then get v tau
is equal to lambda vy

00:55:09.770 --> 00:55:21.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is a heat equation in
terms of variables tau and y.

00:55:21.560 --> 00:55:28.920 align:middle line:84%
And this must solve the original
problem if we substitute back.

00:55:28.920 --> 00:55:33.500 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of
looking for solutions

00:55:33.500 --> 00:55:41.000 align:middle line:84%
that are functions that are
invariant under rescaling time

00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:44.720 align:middle line:90%
and rescaling the space.

00:55:44.720 --> 00:55:53.510 align:middle line:84%
So we must have that u of xt
is equal to u of root alpha x

00:55:53.510 --> 00:56:00.170 align:middle line:84%
and alpha t if we consider
this particular transformation.

00:56:00.170 --> 00:56:05.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, with the choice of alpha
and beta, those are arbitrary.

00:56:05.630 --> 00:56:12.170 align:middle line:84%
We can choose alpha
equal to 1 over t.

00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:18.350 align:middle line:84%
And then that means our u of xt
is equal to u of x over root t

00:56:18.350 --> 00:56:26.390 align:middle line:84%
and 1, which is a function
just of x over root t.

00:56:26.390 --> 00:56:31.790 align:middle line:84%
So we have a transformation
of our original scales

00:56:31.790 --> 00:56:39.840 align:middle line:84%
to a problem where our
solution function v basically--

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:43.470 align:middle line:84%
or our solution function
u has this form.

00:56:43.470 --> 00:56:46.940 align:middle line:84%
And so it's a function
just of one variable.

00:56:46.940 --> 00:56:51.660 align:middle line:84%
And so when this is a
function of one variable,

00:56:51.660 --> 00:56:56.780 align:middle line:84%
if we substitute that
into the heat equation,

00:56:56.780 --> 00:57:00.830 align:middle line:84%
we get the derivative
of h with respect

00:57:00.830 --> 00:57:04.430 align:middle line:84%
to t on the left-hand
side is equal to lambda

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:08.300 align:middle line:84%
times the partial of h with
respect to x, second partial,

00:57:08.300 --> 00:57:10.310 align:middle line:90%
on the right-hand side.

00:57:10.310 --> 00:57:15.200 align:middle line:84%
So those are easily computed
to give that top equation.

00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:19.270 align:middle line:84%
And then we can say, well,
let's stop carrying around

00:57:19.270 --> 00:57:20.690 align:middle line:90%
x over root t everywhere.

00:57:20.690 --> 00:57:26.120 align:middle line:84%
Let's just use y divided by root
t as our variable of interest.

00:57:26.120 --> 00:57:29.950 align:middle line:84%
And we get h prime
of y times y over 2

00:57:29.950 --> 00:57:34.630 align:middle line:84%
equals lambda h double
prime, which is given

00:57:34.630 --> 00:57:37.790 align:middle line:90%
by this last equation here.

00:57:37.790 --> 00:57:44.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, this second derivative of h
divided by the first derivative

00:57:44.110 --> 00:57:48.670 align:middle line:84%
of h is equal to the
derivative of the log

00:57:48.670 --> 00:57:50.980 align:middle line:90%
of the first derivative.

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:54.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's 1 over the first
derivative times the derivative

00:57:54.370 --> 00:57:56.770 align:middle line:90%
of the first derivative.

00:57:56.770 --> 00:58:02.980 align:middle line:84%
So we have that the derivative
of the log of the derivative

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:05.960 align:middle line:84%
of h is equal to
minus lambda over 2y.

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:08.920 align:middle line:84%
And we just integrate both
sides to get the solution

00:58:08.920 --> 00:58:12.460 align:middle line:84%
for h prime is just
a constant times

00:58:12.460 --> 00:58:18.580 align:middle line:84%
the exponential of the
integral of this, which

00:58:18.580 --> 00:58:21.970 align:middle line:90%
is y squared over 4 lambda--

00:58:21.970 --> 00:58:24.680 align:middle line:84%
minus y squared over 4,
and lambda in the exponent.

00:58:24.680 --> 00:58:31.030 align:middle line:84%
And then we integrate this again
to get a solution for h of y.

00:58:31.030 --> 00:58:40.060 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that we're
basically integrating a Gaussian

00:58:40.060 --> 00:58:42.580 align:middle line:90%
density function.

00:58:42.580 --> 00:58:45.500 align:middle line:84%
This is proportional
to a Gaussian density.

00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:48.250 align:middle line:90%
So its antiderivative is--

00:58:48.250 --> 00:58:51.520 align:middle line:84%
or one antiderivative
is actually

00:58:51.520 --> 00:58:55.210 align:middle line:84%
the cumulative normal,
standard normal evaluated

00:58:55.210 --> 00:58:57.910 align:middle line:90%
at y over 2 root 2 lambda.

00:58:57.910 --> 00:59:06.040 align:middle line:84%
So with this, we can
substitute in our value for y

00:59:06.040 --> 00:59:08.120 align:middle line:90%
back in terms of x.

00:59:08.120 --> 00:59:14.310 align:middle line:84%
And we get this formula here
for what the u function is.

00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:17.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, with all of
this development,

00:59:17.830 --> 00:59:24.010 align:middle line:84%
we have a formula for what u
is over the entire time domain.

00:59:24.010 --> 00:59:28.620 align:middle line:84%
But at time 0, it's
the initial condition.

00:59:28.620 --> 00:59:32.400 align:middle line:84%
And so the initial condition is
the limit of this phi function

00:59:32.400 --> 00:59:35.400 align:middle line:90%
as t goes to 0.

00:59:35.400 --> 00:59:44.220 align:middle line:84%
And so when x is negative,
this goes to minus infinity.

00:59:44.220 --> 00:59:48.840 align:middle line:84%
And so we get 0 for the
cumulative distribution function

00:59:48.840 --> 00:59:51.360 align:middle line:90%
when x is less than 0.

00:59:51.360 --> 00:59:55.330 align:middle line:84%
When x is greater than 0,
this goes to plus infinity,

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:56.710 align:middle line:90%
so we get 1.

00:59:56.710 --> 01:00:03.280 align:middle line:84%
And if x equals 0, then
it's always equal to 1/2.

01:00:03.280 --> 01:00:10.950 align:middle line:84%
So we end up having this
function which corresponds

01:00:10.950 --> 01:00:14.010 align:middle line:90%
to the initial conditions.

01:00:14.010 --> 01:00:22.410 align:middle line:84%
And this is the solution
of the heat equation

01:00:22.410 --> 01:00:25.950 align:middle line:84%
when the initial condition
is given by this function.

01:00:25.950 --> 01:00:28.055 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

01:00:28.055 --> 01:00:31.440 align:middle line:90%


01:00:31.440 --> 01:00:32.555 align:middle line:90%
Let's see if we want--

01:00:32.555 --> 01:00:43.290 align:middle line:90%


01:00:43.290 --> 01:00:49.710 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me just write, as
a function of x, I guess,

01:00:49.710 --> 01:00:51.853 align:middle line:90%
the function at 0.

01:00:51.853 --> 01:00:58.230 align:middle line:90%


01:00:58.230 --> 01:01:03.570 align:middle line:84%
And then it's 1/2,
and then it's 1 here.

01:01:03.570 --> 01:01:08.585 align:middle line:84%
So this is the function
u of x0 for this case.

01:01:08.585 --> 01:01:11.090 align:middle line:90%


01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:18.440 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we consider
different linear combinations

01:01:18.440 --> 01:01:25.550 align:middle line:84%
of this solution, we can
actually consider taking--

01:01:25.550 --> 01:01:29.870 align:middle line:84%
instead of u of
x0 equaling this,

01:01:29.870 --> 01:01:38.840 align:middle line:84%
we can consider u of x
corresponding to phi of x

01:01:38.840 --> 01:01:42.050 align:middle line:90%
minus a over root 2 lambda t.

01:01:42.050 --> 01:01:45.110 align:middle line:90%
And so this basic--

01:01:45.110 --> 01:01:50.420 align:middle line:84%
these first two bullets
correspond to shifting this u

01:01:50.420 --> 01:01:55.143 align:middle line:90%
of x0 to a values a and b.

01:01:55.143 --> 01:01:59.360 align:middle line:90%


01:01:59.360 --> 01:02:02.796 align:middle line:90%
And then, with other one, b.

01:02:02.796 --> 01:02:05.840 align:middle line:90%


01:02:05.840 --> 01:02:06.490 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:11.390 align:middle line:90%


01:02:11.390 --> 01:02:16.760 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, the-- so at
a, we have the same.

01:02:16.760 --> 01:02:21.920 align:middle line:84%
And then at b we have the same
of 0 up to b and then a half

01:02:21.920 --> 01:02:23.640 align:middle line:90%
at b and then 1 beyond.

01:02:23.640 --> 01:02:28.310 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have
two step functions

01:02:28.310 --> 01:02:35.810 align:middle line:84%
centered where the steps are
at x equals and at x equals b.

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:42.870 align:middle line:84%
And so both of these u functions
will satisfy the heat equation.

01:02:42.870 --> 01:02:47.780 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider the
difference of the two functions,

01:02:47.780 --> 01:02:50.940 align:middle line:84%
that will also satisfy
the heat equation.

01:02:50.940 --> 01:02:52.880 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look
at the difference

01:02:52.880 --> 01:02:59.670 align:middle line:84%
in the initial conditions
for these two solutions,

01:02:59.670 --> 01:03:07.190 align:middle line:84%
we basically have the indicator
of being between a and b.

01:03:07.190 --> 01:03:11.730 align:middle line:84%
So one function
for b is 1 forever.

01:03:11.730 --> 01:03:17.060 align:middle line:84%
And then the function
for a is 1 up to--

01:03:17.060 --> 01:03:19.670 align:middle line:90%


01:03:19.670 --> 01:03:21.980 align:middle line:90%
or sorry.

01:03:21.980 --> 01:03:24.200 align:middle line:84%
Both functions are
equal to 1 when

01:03:24.200 --> 01:03:27.650 align:middle line:84%
x is greater than b,
in which case we get 0.

01:03:27.650 --> 01:03:32.780 align:middle line:84%
And otherwise, we get 1 when
a is between x and b and 1/2

01:03:32.780 --> 01:03:35.400 align:middle line:90%
otherwise.

01:03:35.400 --> 01:03:41.870 align:middle line:84%
So what we end up having is,
for any initial conditions

01:03:41.870 --> 01:03:48.620 align:middle line:84%
that are representable as a
step function or a sum of step

01:03:48.620 --> 01:03:53.610 align:middle line:84%
functions, we can get
a solution in this way.

01:03:53.610 --> 01:03:57.770 align:middle line:84%
So for our initial
conditions, u0,

01:03:57.770 --> 01:04:02.660 align:middle line:84%
we can basically use limits
of these step functions

01:04:02.660 --> 01:04:08.720 align:middle line:84%
to approximate any
initial condition.

01:04:08.720 --> 01:04:13.900 align:middle line:84%
Well, with stochastic
differential equations

01:04:13.900 --> 01:04:18.460 align:middle line:84%
that we want to
solve, we consider

01:04:18.460 --> 01:04:24.820 align:middle line:84%
generalizations of the
drift and volatility

01:04:24.820 --> 01:04:27.680 align:middle line:90%
parameters of the process.

01:04:27.680 --> 01:04:34.120 align:middle line:84%
And so at the top here
is a generalization

01:04:34.120 --> 01:04:37.030 align:middle line:84%
of simple geometric
Brownian motion,

01:04:37.030 --> 01:04:40.990 align:middle line:84%
where the drift and the
volatilities depend upon time

01:04:40.990 --> 01:04:45.400 align:middle line:84%
as well as the level
of the process x.

01:04:45.400 --> 01:04:48.520 align:middle line:84%
And so what we want
to be able to do

01:04:48.520 --> 01:04:54.610 align:middle line:84%
is understand when solutions
to these differential equations

01:04:54.610 --> 01:04:55.640 align:middle line:90%
exist.

01:04:55.640 --> 01:05:00.680 align:middle line:84%
And there are conditions
that guarantee solutions.

01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:03.310 align:middle line:84%
One of them is a
Lipschitz condition

01:05:03.310 --> 01:05:06.970 align:middle line:84%
which says that the
change in the mu

01:05:06.970 --> 01:05:16.990 align:middle line:84%
function at a fixed time t for
different values of x and y

01:05:16.990 --> 01:05:21.550 align:middle line:84%
plus the change in the
volatility for the same t

01:05:21.550 --> 01:05:23.560 align:middle line:84%
with different x's
and y's, that those

01:05:23.560 --> 01:05:26.350 align:middle line:84%
are bounded by
some constant times

01:05:26.350 --> 01:05:29.230 align:middle line:90%
the difference between x and y.

01:05:29.230 --> 01:05:34.840 align:middle line:90%
And if we have the--

01:05:34.840 --> 01:05:38.990 align:middle line:84%
so this is a space
time condition.

01:05:38.990 --> 01:05:42.910 align:middle line:84%
And with the spatial
growth condition,

01:05:42.910 --> 01:05:48.430 align:middle line:84%
we also want the magnitude
of the drift term

01:05:48.430 --> 01:05:53.530 align:middle line:84%
plus the magnitude of the
squared volatility term

01:05:53.530 --> 01:06:01.220 align:middle line:84%
to be bounded by 1 plus
the square of the x values.

01:06:01.220 --> 01:06:06.130 align:middle line:84%
So what we have here are
conditions for the drift

01:06:06.130 --> 01:06:09.630 align:middle line:84%
not getting too wild
and the volatility not

01:06:09.630 --> 01:06:13.100 align:middle line:84%
getting too wild in order for
there to be solutions to this.

01:06:13.100 --> 01:06:21.660 align:middle line:90%


01:06:21.660 --> 01:06:26.550 align:middle line:84%
A really important theorem
that we won't get into

01:06:26.550 --> 01:06:28.710 align:middle line:84%
but which are dealt
with in advanced

01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:30.810 align:middle line:84%
books on stochastic
calculus deal

01:06:30.810 --> 01:06:36.090 align:middle line:84%
with, the existence
and uniqueness

01:06:36.090 --> 01:06:41.290 align:middle line:84%
of solutions to these stochastic
differential equations.

01:06:41.290 --> 01:06:47.940 align:middle line:84%
And so if we consider
two processes, xt and yt,

01:06:47.940 --> 01:06:52.860 align:middle line:84%
that are both solutions to
the stochastic differential

01:06:52.860 --> 01:07:00.210 align:middle line:84%
equation, then subject to the
conditions that we indicated,

01:07:00.210 --> 01:07:05.190 align:middle line:84%
the two solutions are
actually equal to each other

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:06.940 align:middle line:90%
almost everywhere.

01:07:06.940 --> 01:07:12.750 align:middle line:84%
So we don't get different
limits or different solutions

01:07:12.750 --> 01:07:14.530 align:middle line:90%
to the equations.

01:07:14.530 --> 01:07:18.750 align:middle line:84%
So they're essentially unique,
and "essentially" means that,

01:07:18.750 --> 01:07:25.110 align:middle line:84%
with probability 1,
the paths are the same.

01:07:25.110 --> 01:07:31.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, in solving these stochastic
differential equations,

01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:34.410 align:middle line:84%
a method for solving these that
turns out to be very useful

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:37.150 align:middle line:84%
is a method called
coefficient matching.

01:07:37.150 --> 01:07:42.240 align:middle line:84%
And so let's just illustrate
coefficient matching

01:07:42.240 --> 01:07:44.530 align:middle line:90%
for geometric Brownian motion.

01:07:44.530 --> 01:07:47.160 align:middle line:84%
So here's the
differential equation

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:52.170 align:middle line:90%
for geometric Brownian motion.

01:07:52.170 --> 01:07:56.910 align:middle line:84%
And suppose we have
a solution that's

01:07:56.910 --> 01:08:01.760 align:middle line:84%
some function of time and the
underlying Brownian motion.

01:08:01.760 --> 01:08:12.530 align:middle line:84%
Let's apply the Itô's
formula to x of t.

01:08:12.530 --> 01:08:16.670 align:middle line:84%
And we get dx of t is
equal to this expression

01:08:16.670 --> 01:08:21.290 align:middle line:90%
dt plus this expression dBt.

01:08:21.290 --> 01:08:28.279 align:middle line:84%
And so the--
basically this dx of t

01:08:28.279 --> 01:08:35.810 align:middle line:84%
is applying the chain
rule to f of t and Bt.

01:08:35.810 --> 01:08:40.250 align:middle line:84%
And we can then say,
well, let's match mu

01:08:40.250 --> 01:08:48.890 align:middle line:84%
x of t with this red term
here and match sigma x of t,

01:08:48.890 --> 01:08:51.479 align:middle line:84%
the coefficient
of dBt with df dx.

01:08:51.479 --> 01:08:54.529 align:middle line:90%


01:08:54.529 --> 01:08:59.660 align:middle line:84%
And if we do that, well, the
first or the second equation

01:08:59.660 --> 01:09:04.819 align:middle line:84%
there, sigma f
equals df dx, gives

01:09:04.819 --> 01:09:11.995 align:middle line:84%
a solution of f equaling the
exponential sigma x plus g of t.

01:09:11.995 --> 01:09:21.979 align:middle line:90%


01:09:21.979 --> 01:09:27.410 align:middle line:84%
So this first
equation, mu f equals--

01:09:27.410 --> 01:09:32.860 align:middle line:84%
or sorry, the second equation
is sigma f df dx is equal to--

01:09:32.860 --> 01:09:37.910 align:middle line:90%


01:09:37.910 --> 01:09:39.100 align:middle line:90%
let me just write that out.

01:09:39.100 --> 01:09:47.420 align:middle line:90%


01:09:47.420 --> 01:09:56.270 align:middle line:84%
So we have-- we have sigma
f is equal to df by dx.

01:09:56.270 --> 01:10:04.160 align:middle line:84%
And so we get sigma is
equal to df by dx over f.

01:10:04.160 --> 01:10:15.080 align:middle line:84%
And so this is equal to
d by dx of log of f of x.

01:10:15.080 --> 01:10:20.300 align:middle line:84%
So f of x is going to be the
exponential of the integral

01:10:20.300 --> 01:10:26.360 align:middle line:84%
of sigma with respect to
x And the g of t function

01:10:26.360 --> 01:10:31.650 align:middle line:84%
will just be an arbitrary
constant of that equation.

01:10:31.650 --> 01:10:35.120 align:middle line:84%
And then, if we substitute
this form formula

01:10:35.120 --> 01:10:38.810 align:middle line:84%
for f into the
first equation, we

01:10:38.810 --> 01:10:44.780 align:middle line:84%
get this differential
equation for f.

01:10:44.780 --> 01:10:52.010 align:middle line:84%
And with this differential
equation for f,

01:10:52.010 --> 01:10:55.720 align:middle line:84%
mu f equals g prime f plus
sigma squared over 2f,

01:10:55.720 --> 01:11:00.380 align:middle line:84%
notice that f is a constant
factor in each of those terms.

01:11:00.380 --> 01:11:05.230 align:middle line:84%
So we can eliminate that
constant factor or term,

01:11:05.230 --> 01:11:08.170 align:middle line:84%
the same term,
and we get g prime

01:11:08.170 --> 01:11:11.590 align:middle line:84%
is equal to mu minus
sigma squared over 2.

01:11:11.590 --> 01:11:15.070 align:middle line:84%
So with that, we end
up getting a solution

01:11:15.070 --> 01:11:18.130 align:middle line:84%
for f, which is
the initial value

01:11:18.130 --> 01:11:24.530 align:middle line:84%
x0 times the exponential sigma x
plus this factor times little t.

01:11:24.530 --> 01:11:33.220 align:middle line:84%
And that factor is the adjusted
drift of the geometric Brownian

01:11:33.220 --> 01:11:34.280 align:middle line:90%
motion.

01:11:34.280 --> 01:11:36.460 align:middle line:90%
So we get that--

01:11:36.460 --> 01:11:44.560 align:middle line:84%
we basically get our solution
for geometric Brownian motion

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:47.630 align:middle line:84%
coming out from that
differential equation.

01:11:47.630 --> 01:11:53.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, an interesting
paradox is that the drift

01:11:53.950 --> 01:12:02.230 align:middle line:84%
term is this factor, and
the variance is sigma t.

01:12:02.230 --> 01:12:04.180 align:middle line:84%
Our standard
deviation is sigma t

01:12:04.180 --> 01:12:10.270 align:middle line:84%
for the value of a geometric
Brownian motion process.

01:12:10.270 --> 01:12:13.840 align:middle line:84%
But if our drift
parameter is smaller

01:12:13.840 --> 01:12:18.010 align:middle line:84%
than sigma squared over
2, then this mu star

01:12:18.010 --> 01:12:21.490 align:middle line:90%
is going to be negative.

01:12:21.490 --> 01:12:31.430 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have a
Brownian motion process,

01:12:31.430 --> 01:12:36.760 align:middle line:84%
a geometric Brownian motion
process where on the log scale

01:12:36.760 --> 01:12:42.790 align:middle line:84%
the drift is negative, then
this is going on the log scale

01:12:42.790 --> 01:12:47.300 align:middle line:84%
to go to minus infinity
as t grows large.

01:12:47.300 --> 01:12:53.190 align:middle line:84%
And so the probability that
the geometric Brownian motion

01:12:53.190 --> 01:12:57.300 align:middle line:84%
is smaller than any constant
is going to go to 1 as time

01:12:57.300 --> 01:12:58.060 align:middle line:90%
grows large.

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:03.090 align:middle line:84%
So when the mu terms,
the drift term,

01:13:03.090 --> 01:13:07.900 align:middle line:84%
is small relative to sigma
squared over 2 less than,

01:13:07.900 --> 01:13:18.150 align:middle line:84%
then the likelihood of
this process going to 0

01:13:18.150 --> 01:13:21.030 align:middle line:84%
occurs with probability
approaching 1.

01:13:21.030 --> 01:13:27.210 align:middle line:84%
But complementing that is
that the mean value of x of t

01:13:27.210 --> 01:13:33.810 align:middle line:84%
is growing exponentially at the
rate mu t, exponential rate mu

01:13:33.810 --> 01:13:34.530 align:middle line:90%
t.

01:13:34.530 --> 01:13:37.210 align:middle line:84%
So with geometric
Brownian motion,

01:13:37.210 --> 01:13:41.010 align:middle line:84%
we have this process that
has a mean level that's

01:13:41.010 --> 01:13:44.130 align:middle line:84%
growing arbitrarily
large, but the process

01:13:44.130 --> 01:13:52.450 align:middle line:84%
is, with probability approaching
1, approaching 0 for all paths.

01:13:52.450 --> 01:13:56.860 align:middle line:84%
So that's an
interesting paradox.

01:13:56.860 --> 01:14:02.730 align:middle line:84%
But it appears consistent with
what actually happens often

01:14:02.730 --> 01:14:07.600 align:middle line:84%
in equity markets, where you'll
have some stocks that do really,

01:14:07.600 --> 01:14:09.930 align:middle line:84%
really well and others
that haven't done so well

01:14:09.930 --> 01:14:12.150 align:middle line:90%
and go close to 0.

01:14:12.150 --> 01:14:17.520 align:middle line:84%
On average, maybe there's a mean
growth rate, but many of them

01:14:17.520 --> 01:14:21.600 align:middle line:84%
have ultimate values
that approach 0.

01:14:21.600 --> 01:14:26.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, in the remaining
time, there's

01:14:26.970 --> 01:14:31.210 align:middle line:84%
an introduction here to the
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process,

01:14:31.210 --> 01:14:37.290 align:middle line:84%
which basically is a stochastic
differential equation where

01:14:37.290 --> 01:14:39.970 align:middle line:84%
instead of a
constant drift term,

01:14:39.970 --> 01:14:43.050 align:middle line:84%
we have a drift
rate that depends

01:14:43.050 --> 01:14:46.800 align:middle line:84%
on how far away the process
is from the mean level.

01:14:46.800 --> 01:14:56.040 align:middle line:84%
And so this model is one that
arose with physicists studying

01:14:56.040 --> 01:14:59.940 align:middle line:84%
this, where you look at the
average velocity of molecules

01:14:59.940 --> 01:15:04.720 align:middle line:84%
in a container with a
certain heat or temperature.

01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:08.460 align:middle line:84%
And so particles
should be moving around

01:15:08.460 --> 01:15:12.600 align:middle line:90%
at some average velocity.

01:15:12.600 --> 01:15:18.510 align:middle line:84%
And this model is one way of
trying to characterize that.

01:15:18.510 --> 01:15:21.480 align:middle line:84%
Outside the range
of physics, people

01:15:21.480 --> 01:15:23.890 align:middle line:90%
have used this model in finance.

01:15:23.890 --> 01:15:27.730 align:middle line:84%
Vasicek used it as a model
for interest rate dynamics,

01:15:27.730 --> 01:15:32.050 align:middle line:84%
where there's a mean level that
is expected in the long run,

01:15:32.050 --> 01:15:34.810 align:middle line:84%
and there's
variation about that.

01:15:34.810 --> 01:15:38.920 align:middle line:84%
When the process is
above the average level,

01:15:38.920 --> 01:15:40.930 align:middle line:90%
it tends to drift down.

01:15:40.930 --> 01:15:43.980 align:middle line:84%
And when it's below,
it tends to drift up.

01:15:43.980 --> 01:15:48.120 align:middle line:84%
So with this
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process,

01:15:48.120 --> 01:15:58.110 align:middle line:84%
we can consider a solution
which is a product process.

01:15:58.110 --> 01:16:02.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, you may recall with some
differential equation problems

01:16:02.480 --> 01:16:05.180 align:middle line:84%
where you want to
solve a differential

01:16:05.180 --> 01:16:07.290 align:middle line:90%
equation with two variables.

01:16:07.290 --> 01:16:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And maybe the solution is a
product of one variable times

01:16:11.090 --> 01:16:14.330 align:middle line:90%
a product of another--

01:16:14.330 --> 01:16:17.430 align:middle line:84%
times the a function
of the other variable.

01:16:17.430 --> 01:16:22.490 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, we can think of
a possible solution being given

01:16:22.490 --> 01:16:30.290 align:middle line:84%
by an a of t function times the
integral of the Brownian motion

01:16:30.290 --> 01:16:35.490 align:middle line:84%
process with respect to a little
b of s differentiable function.

01:16:35.490 --> 01:16:40.770 align:middle line:84%
So if we just tentatively
assume this possible solution,

01:16:40.770 --> 01:16:47.060 align:middle line:84%
we can apply the chain rule
to get this first equation.

01:16:47.060 --> 01:16:52.830 align:middle line:84%
dXt is the derivative of a
times the bracketed term,

01:16:52.830 --> 01:16:55.670 align:middle line:84%
then plus a of t
times the derivative

01:16:55.670 --> 01:17:00.410 align:middle line:84%
of the right-hand term
with respect to t.

01:17:00.410 --> 01:17:06.110 align:middle line:84%
And we can then use
coefficient matching

01:17:06.110 --> 01:17:09.750 align:middle line:90%
as per these expressions.

01:17:09.750 --> 01:17:11.900 align:middle line:84%
And at the end of
the day, we end up

01:17:11.900 --> 01:17:17.720 align:middle line:84%
getting our
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process.

01:17:17.720 --> 01:17:22.940 align:middle line:90%
If we start at x0, it--

01:17:22.940 --> 01:17:23.690 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

01:17:23.690 --> 01:17:28.280 align:middle line:84%
If we consider the
process with mu equal to 0

01:17:28.280 --> 01:17:33.290 align:middle line:84%
and initial value of x0, then
it decreases or increases

01:17:33.290 --> 01:17:35.900 align:middle line:90%
exponentially to 0.

01:17:35.900 --> 01:17:42.010 align:middle line:84%
And then the extra
term is this integral

01:17:42.010 --> 01:17:45.530 align:middle line:84%
of Brownian motion with
the exponential term.

01:17:45.530 --> 01:17:48.730 align:middle line:84%
And what's interesting
about this process

01:17:48.730 --> 01:17:53.080 align:middle line:84%
is that it has a limiting
distribution that's

01:17:53.080 --> 01:17:59.300 align:middle line:84%
not like a non-stationary
random walk of Brownian motion.

01:17:59.300 --> 01:18:03.910 align:middle line:84%
But it's centered at
the mean level, which

01:18:03.910 --> 01:18:06.700 align:middle line:84%
was assumed in this
development to be 0,

01:18:06.700 --> 01:18:08.900 align:middle line:90%
and with some constant variance.

01:18:08.900 --> 01:18:13.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's an ergodic
property to this process.

01:18:13.160 --> 01:18:15.880 align:middle line:84%
The long-term
distribution is simply

01:18:15.880 --> 01:18:22.190 align:middle line:84%
this fixed distribution with
constant mean and variance.

01:18:22.190 --> 01:18:27.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, just to finish up, we can
extend stochastic differential

01:18:27.790 --> 01:18:35.050 align:middle line:84%
equations in one space variable
and time to multi dimensions.

01:18:35.050 --> 01:18:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And so we can think of having m
dimensional processes evolving

01:18:42.970 --> 01:18:46.300 align:middle line:90%
for the functions.

01:18:46.300 --> 01:18:49.340 align:middle line:90%
So Xt could be m-dimensional.

01:18:49.340 --> 01:18:55.150 align:middle line:84%
And then we would have a
drift rate corresponding

01:18:55.150 --> 01:18:59.110 align:middle line:84%
to different components of
the dimensions mu 1 to mu m,

01:18:59.110 --> 01:19:03.010 align:middle line:84%
different Brownian motion
terms corresponding

01:19:03.010 --> 01:19:08.710 align:middle line:84%
to m independent
Brownian motions and then

01:19:08.710 --> 01:19:18.040 align:middle line:84%
a covariance matrix reflecting
how similar increments of x

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:23.500 align:middle line:84%
are related to each other in
terms of the covariance terms

01:19:23.500 --> 01:19:25.460 align:middle line:90%
here in this expression.

01:19:25.460 --> 01:19:30.520 align:middle line:84%
So we can have multiple
multi-dimensional stochastic

01:19:30.520 --> 01:19:32.270 align:middle line:90%
differential equations.

01:19:32.270 --> 01:19:35.380 align:middle line:84%
And so here's just
an illustration

01:19:35.380 --> 01:19:41.100 align:middle line:84%
of thinking about mean
reverting velocity and position

01:19:41.100 --> 01:19:42.940 align:middle line:90%
and how that would be set up.

01:19:42.940 --> 01:19:48.360 align:middle line:84%
So the theory of stochastic
differential equations

01:19:48.360 --> 01:19:49.480 align:middle line:90%
is very powerful.

01:19:49.480 --> 01:19:55.510 align:middle line:84%
And it's been exploited a lot
in physics and engineering.

01:19:55.510 --> 01:19:59.670 align:middle line:84%
So with that, let's
see, I just want

01:19:59.670 --> 01:20:02.340 align:middle line:84%
to highlight some
useful references

01:20:02.340 --> 01:20:06.180 align:middle line:84%
for stochastic
differential equations.

01:20:06.180 --> 01:20:09.600 align:middle line:84%
There's this really
excellent monograph

01:20:09.600 --> 01:20:15.130 align:middle line:84%
on by Oksendal which
is very mathematical,

01:20:15.130 --> 01:20:17.340 align:middle line:84%
not necessarily
motivated by finance

01:20:17.340 --> 01:20:21.160 align:middle line:84%
but motivated by physics
problems, as well.

01:20:21.160 --> 01:20:24.000 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
references here

01:20:24.000 --> 01:20:27.100 align:middle line:84%
are really good ones in
quantitative finance.

01:20:27.100 --> 01:20:31.030 align:middle line:84%
So we have our guest
lecturer, John Hull,

01:20:31.030 --> 01:20:35.100 align:middle line:84%
to thank for giving us this
book on Options, Futures,

01:20:35.100 --> 01:20:36.660 align:middle line:90%
and Other Derivatives.

01:20:36.660 --> 01:20:38.240 align:middle line:90%
All right.